# Floods of consecutive zero-length records at a TLS 1.2 server.
#
# RFC 5246 6.2.1: "Implementations MUST NOT send zero-length fragments of
# Handshake, Alert, or ChangeCipherSpec content types. Zero-length fragments
# of Application data MAY be sent as they are potentially useful as a traffic
# analysis countermeasure." Nothing says how many empty records a receiver
# must absorb, so each cap below is a library's own flood defence (OpenSSL's
# comment: "an attacker could send empty records at a faster rate than we can
# process"). The rows pin every backend's reaction rather than loosening to
# an any-of list; suite/client/tls12_empty_record_flood.plzn pins the same
# behaviour from the other side, where every library reacts identically.
#
# Per library, from source and a count sweep in both roles:
#   OpenSSL    (3.6.5) every record whose plaintext is empty, after any
#              decryption, counts toward MAX_EMPTY_RECORDS (32,
#              ssl/record/methods/tls_common.c); the 33rd consecutive one is
#              fatal unexpected_message (SSL_R_RECORD_TOO_SMALL). An
#              unprotected empty handshake record is just such a record, so
#              32 of them pass anywhere in the plaintext part of the handshake.
#   BoringSSL  the same counter (kMaxEmptyRecords = 32, ssl/tls_record.cc,
#              "too many empty fragments"), reset only by a non-empty record.
#              While it waits for the client's ChangeCipherSpec it refuses
#              any handshake record, empty or not (tls_open_change_cipher_spec).
#   LibreSSL   (4.3.3) a zero-length record of any type but application data
#              is SSL_R_BAD_LENGTH, alert unexpected_message, protected or not
#              (ssl_pkt.c); empty application data is skipped up to
#              SSL_MAX_EMPTY_RECORDS (32), and because the test is
#              `count++ > 32` the 34th fails, SSL_R_PEER_BEHAVING_BADLY with
#              no alert.
#   rustls     (0.23.45) a record whose wire length is zero is
#              InvalidEmptyPayload at the deframer, alert decode_error; a
#              protected record that decrypts to nothing is counted whatever
#              its type (ALLOWED_CONSECUTIVE_EMPTY_FRAGMENTS_MAX = 32,
#              conn.rs) and the 33rd is PeerMisbehaved::TooManyEmptyFragments,
#              returned without an alert.
#   Go         (1.27.2) an empty handshake record is unexpected_message at
#              once; empty application data is dropped through
#              retryReadRecord, so the 17th consecutive one exceeds
#              maxUselessRecords (16): unexpected_message "too many ignored
#              records".
#   GnuTLS     (3.8.13) a zero-length record on the wire under the null
#              cipher is GNUTLS_E_UNEXPECTED_PACKET (lib/record.c
#              recv_headers), alert unexpected_message; a protected record
#              that decrypts to nothing is discarded whatever its type, up to
#              DEFAULT_MAX_EMPTY_RECORDS (200), then
#              GNUTLS_E_TOO_MANY_EMPTY_PACKETS, which has no entry in
#              gnutls_error_to_alert and so goes out as internal_error.
#   wolfSSL    (5.9.4) GetRecordHeader refuses a zero-length record of any
#              type but application data with LENGTH_ERROR, sent as
#              record_overflow; a protected handshake record that decrypts to
#              nothing reaches DoHandShakeMsg, whose header parse fails
#              (PARSE_ERROR, no alert); empty application data trips
#              WOLFSSL_MAX_EMPTY_RECORDS (32; the test is `++count >= 32`, so
#              the 32nd) with EMPTY_RECORD_LIMIT_E, and without
#              WOLFSSL_EXTRA_ALERTS that is a close with no alert.
#   Botan      (3.13.0 development HEAD) a zero-length record on the wire is
#              "Received a completely empty record", alert decode_error;
#              before the ClientHello the TLS 1.2 server has no sequence
#              numbers yet, so that alert is not sent and the peer sees only
#              the close. A protected record that decrypts to nothing counts
#              toward Policy::max_useless_records (32), and the 33rd
#              consecutive one is fatal unexpected_message.
#
# Positions: unprotected empty handshake records before the ClientHello,
# after it, between its 64-byte fragments, and after the ClientKeyExchange;
# protected empty handshake records after the client's ChangeCipherSpec; and
# empty application data after the handshake, under an AEAD and a CBC suite
# (a single empty application-data record is tls12_empty_app_data_record).

# The first records on the connection are empty: Botan's TLS 1.2 server has no
# sequence numbers before a ClientHello, so its decode_error is never written.
plzn tls "server/tls12-empty-record-flood/plaintext-handshake-before-client-hello-one-to-thirty-two" {
  rfc 5246 section 6.2.1
  chain: [default]
  uut server {
    versions: [tls12]
  }
  matrix {
    count in [1, 2, 32]
  }
  flow handshake until send ClientHello {
    repeat $count { send empty_record handshake }
    send ClientHello
    recv ServerHello
    recv Certificate
    recv ServerKeyExchange
    recv ServerHelloDone
    send ClientKeyExchange
    send ChangeCipherSpec
    send Finished
    recv ChangeCipherSpec
    recv Finished
    send AppData
    recv AppData
  }
  expect alert fatal/unexpected_message
  expect ok if openssl
  expect ok if boringssl
  expect close if botan
  expect alert fatal/decode_error if rustls
  expect alert fatal/record_overflow if wolfssl
}

